WEBVTT

STYLE
::cue(c.color9CD5FF) { color: #9CD5FF; }

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000 line:5% align:center
<b><c.color9CD5FF>One Pace Addon</c></b>

2
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:16.130
Hmm? Is that rain?

3
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.100
That's interesting.

4
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.560
It looks like a... Oh.

5
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:31.630
<b>Why the Log Pose is Spherical</b>

6
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.030
Grab a holda something!

7
00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.620
Whatever you do, don't let go!

8
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:47.540
Why are there ships
falling out of the sky?

9
00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:50.290
Oh!!

10
00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.630
I know. This is
just an awful dream!

11
00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.710
Great, I love dreams!

12
00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.930
Heads up! I don't think
the party's over yet!

13
00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.010
It won't stop falling!

14
00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.880
The rudder! Turn the rudder!

15
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.200
That'll never work
in these waves!

16
00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:09.530
Luffy, you
gotta protect the ship!

17
00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:10.800
She's taking a beating!

18
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:11.960
Right! Huh?

19
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.090
Usopp?

20
00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:14.380
I cast aside my worries

21
00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.890
and reach deep within
to find my happy place.

22
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.300
And when I open my eyes,

23
00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.520
this serene landscape is
all that will lay before me.

24
00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.120
A skeleton‐dead guy!

25
00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:29.020
Don't throw the nasty
thing at me, you idiot!

26
00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:29.980
They're falling everywhere!

27
00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:31.360
I'm surrounded by death!

28
00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.400
When is this stuff
gonna stop falling?!

29
00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:38.900
All right.

30
00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.160
Why did a ship just
fall out of the sky?

31
00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.350
Captain error?

32
00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:43.360
The sky's clear.

33
00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:44.400
Who knows?

34
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.400
I knew coming to the
Grand Line was a mistake.

35
00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:48.000
It freaks me out.

36
00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:49.450
What's wrong, Nami?

37
00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:50.190
Problem?

38
00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.370
Oh no!

39
00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.160
The Log Pose is broken.

40
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.250
It's pointing into the sky.

41
00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.300
It's not broken.

42
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.130
The Log Pose registers an island
with a strong magnetic pull,

43
00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:00.330
and its needle
is reflecting that.

44
00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:01.110
Huh?

45
00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.280
If the needle is
pointing toward the sky,

46
00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:06.390
then it must be
pointing toward sky island.

47
00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.000
Sky island?

48
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:10.450
Are you serious?

49
00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.360
There's a floating
island up there?

50
00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.370
If there is one,
then it's full of skeletons

51
00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:15.830
and rickety old ships.

52
00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.160
That's ridiculous.

53
00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.150
I don't see anything but sky.

54
00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:21.990
Hmmm...
That's not exactly how it works.

55
00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:24.840
It's not an island above us
but an entire ocean.

56
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:25.930
No way.

57
00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:30.130
I'm still confused.

58
00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.370
Yeah!

59
00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.000
Raise the sails and set
our course for sky island!

60
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.170
Men, are we ready?

61
00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:39.100
Put the rudder down, and point
the bow high into the sky!

62
00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:41.550
– Skyward, ho!
– Skyward, ho!

63
00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:44.230
Did it just hit you?

64
00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:46.750
The fact that, you know,
we can't fly and all that?

65
00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:48.360
To tell you the truth,

66
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:51.440
all I know about sky island
is word of mouth; rumor.

67
00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:52.940
I've never actually seen it.

68
00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:54.080
Of course not!

69
00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:55.250
Because it's a lie!

70
00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:56.760
Islands don't float in the sky.

71
00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.140
Our Log Pose is broken.

72
00:04:58.140 --> 00:04:59.140
That's all it is.

73
00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.060
Not quite, Miss Navigator.

74
00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:03.600
You're trying to fix something
that's not even broken.

75
00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:07.440
We should be trying to find
a way to get to sky island.

76
00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.680
You really need to learn
to trust your instruments.

77
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.300
No matter how crazy things get,

78
00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.720
or how weird the situation
is we find ourselves in,

79
00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.350
never doubt the Log Pose.

80
00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:17.230
Out here, on the water,
this is one of

81
00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.540
the most steadfast
and unbreakable rules.

82
00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.000
What we should be doubting
is the common sense

83
00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:23.230
we all have that tells us

84
00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:27.240
an island cannot exist in
the sky on an ocean of nothing.

85
00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.280
That needle is pointing
toward an island.

86
00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:31.160
I'd bet my life on it.

87
00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:41.000
That's disgusting,

88
00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:43.420
digging through
a dead man's clothes.

89
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.500
That's really gonna
tell you something?

90
00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:49.420
Dunno.

91
00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:52.010
You're into some pretty
weird stuff, Robin.

92
00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.220
Any lady with a fascination

93
00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.390
for dead things is
interesting in my book.

94
00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:57.310
Hmm?

95
00:05:57.350 --> 00:05:58.560
Where'd Luffy go?

96
00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:00.390
Okay, jump!

97
00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:01.420
What kind of trouble

98
00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:03.220
are those two getting
themselves into now?

99
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.450
Probably the bad kind.

100
00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.810
Hey! Looks good as new.

101
00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.570
Except for the
holes on the crown.

102
00:06:09.590 --> 00:06:11.310
Those came from
an external source.

103
00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:12.590
Oh yeah.

104
00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.100
Looks like it could have been
arrows or a spear, or something.

105
00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:16.510
Not quite but good guess.

106
00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:20.100
These are from a medical
procedure called craterization.

107
00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:20.850
Right, doctor?

108
00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:22.470
Mm. An ancient one.

109
00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:23.850
They cut out a
small hole in the skull

110
00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:25.910
to remove a foreign
mass, like a tumor.

111
00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:27.330
And since it leaves
a crater behind,

112
00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:28.920
that's how you get the name.

113
00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.960
Knowing how old of
a technique this is,

114
00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.420
I'd say this man has been
dead at least 200 years.

115
00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:37.740
I can also say
with confidence this man was

116
00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:41.370
in his early thirties
when he died.

117
00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:43.070
It must have been
during his voyage

118
00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:46.670
that he finally succumbed to
the illness, whatever it was.

119
00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.390
Much more than
the rest of his bones,

120
00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:54.060
his teeth have remained
in surprisingly good condition.

121
00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.270
Because he rubbed tar on them.

122
00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:59.210
It was a common
practice in the South Blue

123
00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.210
to keep out
bacterial infections.

124
00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:03.510
And considering
the history of that region,

125
00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.030
I'd suspect that he was
with an exploratory party.

126
00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:09.260
Here it is.

127
00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:11.350
The Briss Kingdom
of the South Blue.

128
00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:16.350
They launched the St. Briss
as a scout ship, 208 years ago.

129
00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:17.070
That's it!

130
00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.130
That's the ship that fell on us.

131
00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.890
Yeah, I recognize
that figurehead out front.

132
00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:23.220
It's probably sailed
the seas above us unmanned

133
00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.760
since the captain and crew died.

134
00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.830
It's amazing what you can tell
from looking at old bones!

135
00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.250
I'm sure you've heard

136
00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.250
that dead men
tell no tales, right?

137
00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:33.730
But everything aroud them does.

138
00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:34.790
If this was a scouting party,

139
00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:37.250
we should find evidence
of that on the St. Briss.

140
00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:40.170
Yeah. Except it's
sinking like a stone.

141
00:07:40.190 --> 00:07:41.480
What are you doing, you idiot?

142
00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:43.240
You can't swim!

143
00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.730
Help! Help me!

144
00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:48.370
Heh. You won't believe it!

145
00:07:48.410 --> 00:07:49.370
I did it!

146
00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.740
I found the most
incredible thing!

147
00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:58.960
– A map of the sky island?
– A map of the sky island?

148
00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:00.550
It's called Skypiea?

149
00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:01.590
That's right.

150
00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:02.430
Then it's true.

151
00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:04.470
There really is
an island up there.

152
00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:09.560
The island's real!
The island's real!

153
00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:10.600
The island's real!

154
00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:11.310
It's a dream!

155
00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:13.180
It's a dream!
It's a dream!

156
00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:16.230
Time to journey up
to sky island!

157
00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:17.440
Hold on, guys.

158
00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:19.600
We don't know how to get there
or if it's even possible.

159
00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:20.560
Not to mention there are

160
00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:23.140
a lot of fake treasure
maps in circulation.

161
00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:26.440
No, I mean. Sure, it's real.

162
00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:30.190
It's gotta be because...
Well, just 'cause.

163
00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:33.240
But the fact is that map doesn't
tell us how to find the place!

164
00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.410
You're the navigator!
Navigate something!

165
00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:36.360
I can't just make things up!

166
00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:37.650
That Nami is a vision

167
00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:40.700
even when she's chewing
someone's head off.

168
00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:43.530
This isn't about me.

169
00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:44.540
It's about reality.

170
00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.560
And the reality is that
we know next to nothing.

171
00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:50.770
What we do know is that we can't
defy gravity and sail upwards.

172
00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:52.020
That's impossible.

173
00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.860
What we need is
more information.

174
00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.860
If a big ship like
that made it up there,

175
00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.030
then the odds are we
can do the same thing.

176
00:08:58.030 --> 00:09:01.280
We just have to find all
the records left on that ship.

177
00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.340
I think the ship's at the
bottom of the ocean by now.

178
00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:06.610
If it sunk, we'll salvage it!

179
00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:08.590
Salvage!

180
00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:10.590
We can't do that!

181
00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:11.760
Salvage? Really?

182
00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.430
Yeah, but we couldn't pull
a ship up from the bottom.

183
00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.820
I think they'll have to come up
with a more refined plan.

184
00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:26.540
Ha ha ha! This is adventure!

185
00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.080
I know I've done
more dangerous things,

186
00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:29.790
but I can't remember what.

187
00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:32.490
Nami, I swear to find
you the answers you seek,

188
00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:34.580
or I will refuse to return!

189
00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:35.620
I'll hold you to that.

190
00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:36.460
You'll be safe.

191
00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:37.840
Those barrels
are my special design.

192
00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:39.560
They've never been busted.

193
00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:46.360
Good luck! Be careful!

194
00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:48.450
We'll be pulling for ya.

195
00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:55.500
Hey, look at that!

196
00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:56.750
This is Chopper here.

197
00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:58.120
Can you guys call off?

198
00:09:58.460 --> 00:09:59.340
This is Luffy,

199
00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.580
and there's a ton of
monsters down here.

200
00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:03.830
We're on a nest
of giant sea snakes.

201
00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.520
This is Sanji,
and they're staring at me.

202
00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:07.380
Keep going.

203
00:10:07.430 --> 00:10:08.430
They'll be fine, Usopp.

204
00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.090
Don't worry.

205
00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:12.440
And Chopper.

206
00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:14.020
Hold that brake tightly.

207
00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:14.820
Right.

208
00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:16.970
I'm so glad they
didn't make me go.

209
00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.550
– Salvage!
– Salvage!

210
00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:25.470
– Salvage the sea!
– Salvage the sea!

211
00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:28.310
– Salvage!
– Salvage!

212
00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.890
– Salvage the sea!
– Salvage the sea!

213
00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:32.530
What's all that noise?

214
00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:38.820
– Salvage the sea! Salvage!
– Salvage the sea! Salvage!

215
00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:45.450
– Salvage the sea! Salvage!
– Salvage the sea! Salvage!

216
00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:48.580
Company, halt!

217
00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:51.050
– Aye aye, sir!
– Aye aye, sir!

218
00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.850
Are we on top of
the place where the ship sank?

219
00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:58.190
– Aye aye, Captain, sir!
– Aye aye, Captain, sir!

220
00:10:59.350 --> 00:11:01.190
When they say captain and sir,

221
00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.220
they're talking about me!

222
00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:08.230
<b>"Salvage King" Masira
Masira Pirates
Bounty: 23 Million Belly</b>

223
00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:05.390
Prepare to raise the ship!

224
00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:07.300
What lies on the
bottom now belongs

225
00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:09.100
to the King of All Salvagers!

226
00:11:09.210 --> 00:11:12.590
Captain of the
Masira Pirates, Masira!

227
00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:19.180
Great. A boat of unstable
men led by an unstable monkey.

228
00:11:19.220 --> 00:11:20.780
Hey! What are
you guys doing here?

229
00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:24.030
In case you didn't know,
all these waters belong to me.

230
00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:25.450
They do?

231
00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:26.580
Yeah, that's right!

232
00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:27.530
Every vessel that sinks

233
00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:30.080
in these parts
automatically becomes mine.

234
00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:31.120
You're not trying
to take something

235
00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:33.530
that belongs to me, are you?

236
00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:35.490
So he's going
to bring it to the surface.

237
00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:36.860
Easier for us.

238
00:11:36.860 --> 00:11:38.990
But not for the
three guys already in the water.

239
00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:41.450
We should just keep our mouths
shut and let him do it.

240
00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:43.660
Stop mumbling and answer me!

241
00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:44.960
Are you people trying to yank

242
00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:47.380
one of my salvages
out from under me?

243
00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:48.390
Excuse me!

244
00:11:48.390 --> 00:11:50.150
Can I ask you a question first?

245
00:11:50.150 --> 00:11:51.900
You've got questions for me?

246
00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:54.440
Fine. Sure. Whatever.
Ask me anything.

247
00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.900
Are all of you
tough, good‐looking men

248
00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:57.570
gonna salvage that ship?

249
00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:58.650
What?

250
00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:02.090
Thank you.

251
00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:04.170
I see you know what
a real man looks like.

252
00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:05.820
What are you talking about?

253
00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:07.490
You called us all
tough and good‐looking.

254
00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:08.690
That includes me.

255
00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:09.820
Well, of course!

256
00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:11.920
Stop! You're making me blush!

257
00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:15.050
This is disturbing.

258
00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:17.880
So, are you gonna
salvage that thing or what?

259
00:12:17.930 --> 00:12:18.880
Of course!

260
00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:21.210
I'm not the King of All
Salvagers for nothing!

261
00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:23.970
If there's a sunken ship
before me, then I'll salvage it!

262
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:27.120
If it's not sunken, then I'll
sink it and salvage it later.

263
00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:29.820
Now, I know how impressed you
are, but we have to save it.

264
00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:31.440
There's salvaging at hand!

265
00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:32.400
Loser.

266
00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:34.780
Do you mind if we watch
you big strong men work?

267
00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:36.030
Huh?

268
00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:38.780
Don't tell me you've never seen
a professional salvage before.

269
00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:39.780
Haha!

270
00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:42.950
You just watch us till
your heart's content!

271
00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:43.960
That's that.

272
00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:45.870
Now we just have to wait
and see what happens.

273
00:12:46.210 --> 00:12:47.080
Below.

274
00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:49.840
In the water, there's a...

275
00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:51.710
Captain, we have a problem!

276
00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:52.460
What is it?

277
00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:53.340
Something happened to the guys

278
00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:55.870
who went into the water
to set the cradle.

279
00:12:55.910 --> 00:12:57.760
Was it one of those
Sea Kings again?

280
00:12:57.990 --> 00:12:59.200
I don't think so.

281
00:12:59.200 --> 00:13:01.170
It looks like someone went to
town on them with their fists.

282
00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:02.290
What?

283
00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:04.840
You mean someone else besides
our guys are down there?

284
00:13:04.870 --> 00:13:05.870
I'm gonna kill 'em.

285
00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:08.590
You may not get the chance.

286
00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:10.050
Listen up, you!

287
00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:14.390
There's someone else
down there, so be careful!

288
00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:16.030
Oh, sure.

289
00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.970
– What a moron.
– What a moron.

290
00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:19.310
I want all hands
setting the cradle!

291
00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:21.730
The ship comes up in 20 minutes!

292
00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:23.700
We've got salvaging to do!

293
00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:26.820
Aye aye, Captain!

294
00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:42.000
Okay, he's waving at us.

295
00:13:42.040 --> 00:13:43.500
Smile and wave back, I guess.

296
00:13:43.500 --> 00:13:45.260
Just act normal.

297
00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:46.770
Doing my best.

298
00:13:47.360 --> 00:13:48.570
Don't get stage‐fright, fellas.

299
00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:49.480
They're observers.

300
00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:50.700
They want to see something new.

301
00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:52.740
So show them how it's done.

302
00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:54.580
If you're still nervous,

303
00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:56.580
try picturing them
in their underwear.

304
00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:58.710
Aye aye, Captain!

305
00:13:58.730 --> 00:14:00.400
Drop the cradle!

306
00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:01.700
– Aye aye!
– Aye aye!

307
00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:07.480
Keep your eyes
peeled for those intruders!

308
00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:09.490
Aye aye, sir!

309
00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:10.330
These guys really take

310
00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:12.500
their salvaging
seriously don't they?

311
00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:14.030
I think they're showing off

312
00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:15.690
because their captain
has a crush on me.

313
00:14:15.690 --> 00:14:17.960
Move it, men!
I want this done fast!

314
00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:20.110
– Aye aye, sir!
– Aye aye, sir!

315
00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:30.540
Hmm...

316
00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:56.480
Hmm...

317
00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:28.260
Hmm...

318
00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:33.600
Zoro.

319
00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:34.890
Look.

320
00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:01.940
Cradle set is locked in place.

321
00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:06.030
What was that?

322
00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:07.570
Huh?

323
00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:10.460
Uh, so why such a big monkey?

324
00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:13.050
There on the bow of the ship.

325
00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:13.760
Oh, that.

326
00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:16.090
Hehehe. You like?

327
00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:18.010
It's a special prow I designed.

328
00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:19.050
It aids in our salvage.

329
00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:20.200
Just watch.

330
00:16:21.550 --> 00:16:23.600
You're gonna love this!

331
00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.100
Lower the Barco‐hunter!

332
00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:27.800
– Aye aye, sir!
– Aye aye, sir!

333
00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:32.190
– Monkey, monkey, monkey!
– Monkey, monkey, monkey!

334
00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:33.980
Monkey, monkey, monkey!
Monkey, monkey, monkey!

335
00:16:34.020 --> 00:16:35.650
– Monkey, monkey, monkey!
– Monkey, monkey, monkey!

336
00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:39.490
Monkey, monkey, monkey!
Monkey, monkey, monkey!

337
00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:42.470
– Monkey, monkey, monkey!
– Monkey, monkey, monkey!

338
00:16:42.470 --> 00:16:44.090
Wow! Awesome!

339
00:16:44.090 --> 00:16:45.510
Completely amazing!

340
00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:47.010
Are you kidding?

341
00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:52.350
Forty meters to the target.

342
00:16:52.390 --> 00:16:55.150
Thirty meters.

343
00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:56.270
Twenty.

344
00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:58.940
Ten.

345
00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:03.650
Barco‐hunter joint locked!

346
00:17:03.650 --> 00:17:04.350
Excellent!

347
00:17:04.350 --> 00:17:06.130
Now it's time for me to blow!

348
00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:08.430
– Aye aye, sir!
– Aye aye, sir!

349
00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:11.610
Wait. There's no way.

350
00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:19.070
He's actually going to use his
breath to bring up that ship?

351
00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:22.110
That's ridiculous!

352
00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:36.700
Is that a bubble?

353
00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:49.090
The body of
the ship is off the floor.

354
00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:51.760
All right! Haul her up!

355
00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:54.010
– Aye aye, sir!
– Aye aye, sir!

356
00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:56.110
Let's hustle, men!

357
00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:57.430
Send more air!

358
00:17:57.780 --> 00:17:59.940
Aye aye, sir!

359
00:18:08.730 --> 00:18:09.690
Captain!

360
00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:11.660
There's a problem with
the underwater crew!

361
00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:13.230
What?

362
00:18:13.230 --> 00:18:14.150
Talk to me!

363
00:18:14.280 --> 00:18:16.160
What's happening down there?!

364
00:18:16.160 --> 00:18:17.620
Is it the intruders?

365
00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:19.610
Yes, sir! They're in the ship!

366
00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:28.870
How dare you lay a hand
on my hard‐working men!

367
00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:31.380
Intruders!

368
00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:39.240
Uh, you know, we're
not gonna take your picture

369
00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:40.060
or anything.

370
00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:41.310
You're not?

371
00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:43.020
He was giving us a photo op?

372
00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:47.120
Help us! Someone please, help us!

373
00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:48.330
I'll be right down.

374
00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:50.540
Continue with the
salvaging operation men!

375
00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:52.580
– Aye aye, sir!
– Aye aye, sir!

376
00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:15.250
Monkey punch!

377
00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:19.330
The salvage looks good.

378
00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:23.680
Check it out.

379
00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:25.850
We don't even need
the barrels now.

380
00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:27.850
Well, I don't know how
they pumped this much air

381
00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:29.560
into a downed ship.

382
00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:31.730
But what I really want
to know is who they are

383
00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:33.480
and why they're trying
to salvage this thing.

384
00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:35.270
I wonder if something
bad happened to Nami

385
00:19:35.270 --> 00:19:37.230
and Robin up on the surface.

386
00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:40.660
I've tried calling them,
but they won't respond.

387
00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:42.720
Who do you guys think you are?

388
00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:45.250
Trying to muscle in on my turf?

389
00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:50.550
Nice makeup.

390
00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:53.780
Thanks! I feel it adds a
refined quality to my face.

391
00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:55.740
Oh, yeah.
It certainly adds something.

392
00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:57.610
What the hell are
you talking about?

393
00:19:57.670 --> 00:19:59.460
What do you mean?

394
00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:01.070
What he means
to say is, who are you?

395
00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:01.820
You're right.

396
00:20:01.820 --> 00:20:02.860
How rude of me.

397
00:20:02.870 --> 00:20:05.410
Sometimes I get caught up
in the details of life.

398
00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.040
It's one of my shortcomings.

399
00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.450
Come on!
Just tell us who you already!

400
00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:15.750
There's something
else down there.

401
00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:17.460
It's huge!

402
00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.520
Anybody just wanna run?

403
00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:25.450
Yeah, all you guys

404
00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:28.270
from the East Blue have the
best sense of humor!

405
00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:29.930
Well, I don't really
know about all that.

406
00:20:29.930 --> 00:20:32.770
Nothing compares to a
one ton gorilla in makeup.

407
00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:33.940
Oh, shut your mouth.

408
00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:35.770
You know I look good.

409
00:20:56.460 --> 00:20:57.500
Does anyone else see

410
00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:00.970
the monster the size of
an island in front of us?
